WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.480
- [Reporter] Before the pandemic,
San Francisco's downtown

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.820
was one of the most dynamic
commercial districts

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:06.690
in the country,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.570
home to some of the
nation's biggest retailers.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.710
But today, many of those
businesses have abandoned the city.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.610
These closures follow the
disappearance or scaling back

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.460
of many of the city's most
prominent corporate tenants

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.590
like Facebook and Salesforce.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.420
- Downtown San Francisco today

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.480
is really a shell of its former self.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.600
- The way I see San Francisco
from afar, it's amazing.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.440
But once you get into the
nitty-gritty, like, downtown

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.790
and get to the heart of it,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.600
it loses its glamour
that it has, you know?

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.400
- [Reporter] But while
a national consensus

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.580
seems to have formed around
downtown San Francisco's demise,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.390
some investors and businesses
think the worst is over

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.510
and that the city is now
primed for a turnaround.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.210
- San Francisco's always
been a boom and bust town.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.010
- There is a perception problem.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.340
- [Reporter] Some retailers,
like IKEA, are moving in

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.340
and San Francisco's office
market is also seeing progress

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.460
for the first time since
the onset of the pandemic

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.850
thanks in part to the
arrival of AI companies.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.240
But are these recent positive
developments really signs

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.950
that the worst is over?

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.240
Today, almost 32% of San
Francisco's office space is vacant,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.980
which is more than seven times the rate

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.420
before the pandemic hit

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.973
and the biggest increase
of any major US city.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.050
Cyrus Sanandaji is the managing director

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.910
at Presidio Bay Ventures,

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.220
a Bay Area commercial
real estate developer

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.010
that sees opportunity in the down market.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.530
Recently, Presidio Bay Ventures acquired

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.690
the office tower 60 Spear Street

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.220
in the city's financial district

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.440
for about $41 million,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.850
or some 62% less than it sold for in 2014.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.250
- So we purchased 60 Spear

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.490
as an opportunistic investment unlevered

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.890
with a really long-term horizon,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.160
understanding that San Francisco
is a boom and bust town

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.640
and looking at historical market cycles.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.640
We've not seen basis this
low in over 10 years.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.930
With the amount of vacancy
that's in the building,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.220
70% today,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.310
we're able to really
reimagine the entire building.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.440
- [Reporter] 60 Spear's
acquisition represents

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.900
a new positive trend
within San Francisco's

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.060
downtown commercial real estate market.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.670
After a long stretch when
the city's office buildings

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.080
couldn't attract buyers,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.240
sales are slowly materializing.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.000
- The industry around
artificial intelligence

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.480
could be a lifeline for a
struggling American city.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.210
- [Reporter] Recently, scores
of AI companies have begun

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.650
leasing space in the city,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.660
suggesting that San Francisco's appeal

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.060
as a tech hub hasn't evaporated.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.070
- There's been a boom in
artificial intelligence

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.120
in San Francisco,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.000
and in fact, the top 20
companies in the world,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.320
eight are in San Francisco,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.110
with over 80 companies total

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.230
of the 223 companies for
artificial intelligence,

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.480
they're here in San Francisco.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.430
- [Reporter] Despite the new AI tenants

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.890
and signs of life in the
commercial real estate market,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.660
serious challenges remain,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.843
especially concerning public
safety and homelessness.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.230
- San Francisco's downtown
before the pandemic

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.570
was just absolutely bustling.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.730
You know, I work on California Street

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.710
and there were just throngs of people.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.300
It was like Manhattan.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.610
It was like the city of gold.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:21.840
- [Reporter] Since the pandemic,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.740
many major US cities saw their
office vacancy rates balloon,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.290
but San Francisco is
the leader nationally.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.000
- There's really been a perfect storm,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.060
which has kind of imploded
downtown San Francisco

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.320
over the last four years.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.100
One is the high housing costs
really forced a lot of workers

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.320
to work many, you know, miles away.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.640
Because San Francisco became

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.670
so concentrated with technology,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:45.960
technology is the one industry

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.410
which got hit harder by
remote work than any other,

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.140
and that's why here in
downtown San Francisco,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.680
these office buildings that
surround me are mostly empty.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.960
And then finally, there's been the problem

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.580
of crime and homelessness,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.800
which has been around for years,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.070
but it really festered and
got worse during the pandemic.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.410
- [Reporter] David Lee is
a local restaurant owner

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.320
who has lived in San Francisco since 2005.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.180
This year, Lee decided to close

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.580
his two profitable taco restaurants

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.580
after multiple robberies
and instances of vandalism.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.703
- So this is my old shop,
this is the old taqueria,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.060
but kind of sad to see it all gated up.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.890
- [Reporter] Lee is one
of many business owners

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.050
who have become fed up with San Francisco,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.670
not just because of the petty crime,

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.220
but also because of a
lack of foot traffic.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.200
- When I first started my business,

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.020
I was averaging about $3,000 at lunch

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.480
and then about $3,000 at dinner time

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.300
and I was only open for six hours a day.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.560
It was a short period of time.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.090
But beginning of this year,

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.137
I was averaging around
like a thousand dollars,

00:04:47.137 --> 00:04:49.050
$1,500 a day.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.870
Once upon a time, this was
like the busiest street.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:52.980
It used to be thriving,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.183
but now there's nobody
walking to or from work.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.370
It's sad.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.410
- [Reporter] Lee is now
planning to move his business

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.170
to Las Vegas, Nevada, or Austin, Texas.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.310
- [David] 'Cause I've
been in San Francisco

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.540
for such a long time

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.640
and I've just kind of
seen the decline of it

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.310
that I'm just ready to leave.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.460
- Downtown San Francisco
is absolutely crucial

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.440
to the health of San Francisco.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.270
It accounts for 75% of the
city's budget and revenues.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.370
Most of the jobs are in
downtown San Francisco.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.670
Without these buildings full,
the revenue's gonna go down.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.640
So for the city to be healthy as a whole,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.140
the downtown has to come back.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.890
- San Francisco downtown as we know it

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.373
is not coming back.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.060
And you know what? That's okay.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.670
- [Reporter] In February,
Mayor London Breed laid out

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.060
her vision for revitalizing
San Francisco's downtown.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.990
- Empty office buildings
have fueled dire predictions

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.190
about economic doom

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:57.840
and screaming headlines
about the death of downtown.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.300
But let's keep some perspective here.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.860
In 1907, downtown was
mostly rubble and ash.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.740
That's considerably
worse than today's shift

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.630
in how people are working.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.660
We do need to, you
know, just be realistic.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.510
Retail is not what it used to be.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:16.530
Things have changed.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:17.363
- [Reporter] Breed is working

00:06:17.363 --> 00:06:19.170
to streamline the permitting process

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:20.910
for new downtown businesses,

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.100
as well as creating more flexible zoning

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.010
to diversify the uses
of downtown properties.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.000
- So our economic roadmap to
recovery for San Francisco

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.540
includes a lot of legislative changes

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.500
to remove the barriers that
make it difficult for us

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.500
to convert a lot of our office
space from office to housing.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.970
So we think that it's gonna lead

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:48.150
to some more housing options.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.680
We think it's gonna lead to
other uses besides office space.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.690
And our hope is that we start

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.670
to reimagine what downtown can be

00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:00.780
other than nine-to-five financial district

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.580
or even just retail.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.530
And we're gonna focus hard on making sure

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.990
that people understand
what we are as a city,

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.850
not what others say we are.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.800
- [Reporter] Breed also
pushed back against

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.740
what she said were
unfair characterizations

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.120
in the national media.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.370
- Tonight, Jesse Watter's
prime time is declaring

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.620
San Francisco has hit rock bottom.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.930
- There's concern that
San Francisco could turn

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.700
into a so-called zombie city

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.800
with its downtown hollowed out.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.150
- It's not to say

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.640
that, you know, we don't
have our challenges.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.020
We're a major city.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.850
Major cities have challenges

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.090
with, you know, crime and
violence and other issues.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.820
So I do think that, you
know, there's a perception

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.410
that when you see these videos

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.850
or you hear about what's going on,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.020
it's like, "Oh my goodness,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:44.610
what's going on in San Francisco?"

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.310
And now what we're finding
is someone else has tried

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.710
to define what San Francisco is

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.660
and we have to change that narrative.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.600
- The mayor has talked
about San Francisco having

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:55.920
a perception problem

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.350
where people in other
parts of the country think

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.970
it's basically a hellhole.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.550
I think the reality is not true.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.890
I mean, there are parts of San Francisco

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:08.490
which are in bad shape for sure,

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.490
but for the most part, you
know, it's a beautiful city.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.180
- I'd say if we're not at
the bottom, we're very close.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.690
From our perspective and
with our investment horizon,

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.840
we don't need to be perfect
and time the exact bottom,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.310
though, we feel we're there.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.040
- San Francisco is growing.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:30.510
Companies are expanding and
making commitments to the city.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:34.170
But it's not just in the
downtown office space.

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.300
Companies want different things now

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.630
and so we have to make
sure that we are adjusting

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.210
to what the demand is

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.247
in so many different arenas.

00:08:44.247 --> 00:08:46.830
(bright music)

